Kural Graph
===========

The Tamil text of the Tirukkural is ancient and in the public domain. This
project's digital copy starts from:

  tk120404/thirukkural (https://github.com/tk120404/thirukkural)
  Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.

Lines from that dataset have been corrected: see
content/data/clean/tamil-fixes.yaml in https://github.com/carun/kural-graph for
every change. Only the Tamil lines,
their numbering, chapter names and its romanisation (for search only) are used.
Its commentaries and translations are not included.

The English readings were written for this project by an AI (Anthropic's
Claude), from the Tamil and parimēlaḻagar's commentary.

The project's own annotations and readings are licensed under Creative Commons
Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International: see content/LICENSE in
https://github.com/carun/kural-graph. Commercial use needs the owner's permission:
ask at https://arunc.dev/contact/. The texts described here keep their own terms,
and the code is under the MIT licence.

parimēlaḻagar's commentary is about seven hundred years old. The Tamil shown
here is from Tamil Wikisource
(https://ta.wikisource.org/wiki/திருக்குறள்_பரிமேலழகர்_உரை), whose text is available
under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike licence. Wiki markup, editors'
word lists and footnote numbers have been removed.

V. V. S. Aiyar's 1916 English translation is taken from the Standard Ebooks
edition (https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/thiruvalluvar/the-kural/v-v-s-aiyar),
which is believed to be in the United States public domain.

G. U. Pope's English verse (The 'Sacred' Kurral of Tiruvalluva-Nayanar, 1886) was
read from the scans at https://archive.org/details/tiruvalluvanayan00tiruuoft and
https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.43871. W. H. Drew and J. Lazarus's
English prose (The Kural of Tiruvalluvar, 1885) was read from the scan at
https://archive.org/details/kuraloftiruvallu00tirurich, and Kurals 604 and 761 to
765 from a later reprint at
https://archive.org/details/xkae-thirukkural-w.-h.-drew-john-lazarus. Both are in
the public domain. Plain printing errors have been corrected; see
content/data/clean/pope.json and content/data/clean/drew-lazarus.json in the same
repository for every change.

mu. varaḍarāsaṉār's திருக்குறள் தெளிவுரை and mayilai civamuṭṭu's திருக்குறள், இனிய
எளிய உரை are from Tamil Wikisource
(https://ta.wikisource.org/wiki/திருக்குறள்_தெளிவுரை,_மு._வரதராசன்,
https://ta.wikisource.org/wiki/திருக்குறள்,_இனிய_எளிய_உரை), whose text is available
under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike licence. Wiki markup has been
removed, and slips in the first were corrected against its scan. Tamil Wikisource
lists mayilai civamuṭṭu's works as nationalised by the Government of Tamil Nadu.
The Government announced the nationalisation of mu. varaḍarāsaṉār's works in 2009.

ṭēvanēyap pāvāṇar's திருக்குறள் தமிழ் மரபுரை (first published 1967) is from the
2009 reprint in பாவாணர் தமிழ்க் களஞ்சியம், volumes 1 to 4, in the Tamil Digital
Library (https://archive.org/details/tdl.48404-1-1 and its three companions). His
works were nationalised by the Government of Tamil Nadu in 2006. The reprint's
hyphen for the letter ய் has been restored.

maṇakkudavar's commentary is a medieval work. The text here is from Project
Madurai (https://www.projectmadurai.org/pm_etexts/utf8/pmuni0650.html), rearranged
so that each comment sits under its couplet's usual number. That file's header:

  tirukkuRaL verses with maNakkuTavar urai (in unicode format)
  திருவள்ளுவர் அருளிச் செய்த
  திருக்குறள் மூலமும் மணக்குடவர் உரையும்
  (திருவள்ளுவமாலையுடன்)
  tirukkuRaL verses with the urai/commentary
